    "https://apply.interfolio.com/141631": {
      "error": "RuntimeError: Interfolio position has no title (closed)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195729": {
      "title": "Associate Professor Adjunct in Sculpture",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Art Location: New Haven, CT The Yale University School of Art invites applications for a full-time Associate Professor Adjunct faculty appointment in Sculpture to begin July 1, 2027. Candidates must hold an MFA or equivalent degree. The candidate will serve as the Director of Graduate Studies for a designated period of three years with the potential for renewal, while remaining active professionally and teaching in a rigorous academic environment. We seek a working artist with a primary focus on sculpture or its expanded category. The candidate should have an active and engaged exhibition record, as well as a strong technical background and conceptual grounding in the medium and history of sculpture. A minimum of 5 years college-level teaching and service experience, and an ongoing, excellent, and dynamic art practice is required. A knowledge of the discourses around contemporary art and the ability to address works across disciplines is necessary. A fully engaged presence on campus is required.  The successful candidate will collaborate with the Department’s two ladder faculty members to shape the future of the Sculpture area at the School of Art. This role includes the teaching of graduate and undergraduate level courses, advising graduate and undergraduate students, overseeing master’s theses, participating in critique sessions, mentoring students, serving on School of Art committees, and actively contributing to and participating in the school’s vibrant co-curricular programming. Reporting to the dean, the Director of Graduate Studies in Sculpture oversees curriculum development, scheduling courses, coordinating departmental budgets, and - in conjunction with the Dean - hiring and evaluating part-time or per-course faculty to maintain curricular standards. The ideal candidate should have a vision and passion for graduate education in studio art in the liberal arts / research university context; be an empathetic and experienced advisor to students from different backgrounds and working in various mediums; and have administrative experience. A record of educational service that addresses multiple modalities of accessibility, content, and teaching is a crucial criterion for consideration. To apply, please submit the following materials online through Interfolio. · A letter of application · Curriculum vitae with relevant professional and teaching experience · Names and contact information of three references · A sample syllabus for an undergraduate sculpture course, and a course proposal for a graduate seminar · 20 images of recent work Review of applications begins January 1, 2027.  The University is committed to basing judgments concerning the admission, education, and employment of individuals upon their qualifications and abilities and seeks to attract to its faculty, staff, and student body qualified persons from a broad range of backgrounds and perspectives."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195743": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Psychological Medicine Inpatient Consultation Services",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Psychiatry Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Psychiatry at Yale School of Medicine is recruiting a full-time psychiatrist to join our nationally recognized Psychological Medicine Section at Yale New Haven Hospital, the birthplace of proactive Consultation-Liaison (C-L) psychiatry. The position is for general C-L psychiatry at Yale New Haven Hospital. We seek a board-certified/board-eligible psychiatrist with fellowship training in C-L psychiatry or addiction psychiatry with experience in consult psychiatry. The successful candidate will serve as an attending psychiatrist on the Psychiatry Consult Service, providing expert consultation to medically and surgically hospitalized patients at YNHH. Clinical work is grounded in team-based care and close partnership with consulting services, with opportunities to contribute to both traditional and proactive C-L models. This faculty role is ideal for a clinician-educator who thrives in a highly collaborative academic environment with robust teaching opportunities across medical student, resident, and fellowship levels. The Psychological Medicine Section includes an inpatient psychiatric consultation service providing psychiatric consultation to Yale New Haven Hospital (YNHH), a busy medical center with 1,541 beds and16 integrated psychiatry and behavioral health programs in diverse medical and surgical outpatient settings. Yale New Haven Hospital is the major teaching hospital for the Yale School of Medicine and the Yale School of Nursing, a Level I trauma center and a Magnet hospital, which consistently ranks among the best in the US. A 204-bed brand-new Adams Neuroscience Center is scheduled to open in 2027.  (. Yale New Haven Hospital includes the Yale New Haven Psychiatric Hospital, a 118-bed acute-care psychiatric facility with emergency services, inpatient programs, specialized step-down intensive outpatient/day hospital programs for adolescents and adults, and Interventional Psychiatric Services. This position carries a faculty appointment within the Department of Psychiatry at the assistant or associate professor level, depending upon the candidate’s qualifications and experience. Yale University offers Yale Signature Benefits, including but not limited to: ·         Medical coverage through Yale Health and Aetna ·         Dental coverage through Delta Dental ·         Vision coverage options through Eyemed ·         Yale University Retirement Account Plan ·         Paid time off ·         Disability coverage ·         Life insurance ·         Mortgage loan program ·         Commuter benefits ·         Yale Homebuyer program ·         College tuition benefit plan Yale ·         Advantage discount program (Discounts for Activities, Travel, services, shopping, etc.) The salary range listed below reflect the anticipated compensation range for all ranks included in this recruitment: $252,000 - $277,000 Living in/near New Haven New Haven is recognized as a city of innovation, culture, diversity, and prosperity. It is located between Boston and New York on Long Island Sound, and has outstanding cultural amenities (museums, theater, music, and sports), award-winning restaurants (including several of the nation’s best pizza parlors), boutique shops and national brands, quaint neighborhoods and bustling downtown areas with a variety of living options. New Haven is accessible by plane, train, and automobile, and is easy to navigate on foot, by bicycle, or bus. A free shuttle service provided by Yale covers many areas in the city. Board-certified/board-eligible psychiatrist with fellowship training in C-L psychiatry or addiction psychiatry with experience in consult psychiatry. To apply to this position please use the following link apply.interfolio.com/195743 Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you have questions about these job opportunities, please contact Hun Millard, Vice Chief of Psychiatry, via email at hun.millard@yale.edu for more information."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195786": {
      "title": "Hellenic Studies Program: Postdoctoral Associate, 2027-2028",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: MacMillan Center Location: New Haven, CT The Yale University Hellenic Studies Program seeks applications for one Postdoctoral Associate for the 2027-2028 academic year, chosen for the promise of their research. Housed in the MacMillan Center for International and Area Studies, the interdisciplinary Hellenic Studies Program supports and coordinates the teaching and study of topics related to modern Greece at Yale. The postdoctoral associate will be required to continue research or writing on topics related to Hellenic Studies and contribute to the development of planning, events, and conferences for the Hellenic Studies Program. Ideal candidates should work on topics related to Modern Greece and Modern Greek studies, including various disciplines within the humanities, social sciences, sciences, engineering, and medicine. The salary for this position is commensurate with experience and complies with the Yale Office of Postdoctoral Affairs compensation policy and includes a benefits package . Graduate students who are working on their doctoral degrees may apply, but fellowship recipients must have completed their PhD dissertations and present proof that they have received their doctoral degree. Applicants must have completed their PhD within 5 years of beginning at Yale. Postdoctoral Associates are expected to be in full-time residence in New Haven, Connecticut, for the full academic year so that they can take an active part in the intellectual exchange with members of the Yale community. While applications will be accepted on a rolling basis, those received by February 1, 2027 will receive priority review. Only submissions through Interfolio will be accepted. Application Materials Required: ·         Cover letter ·         Curriculum vitae ·         Research statement (no more than 1000 words) ·         Writing sample - No more than 30 pages.  The sample can be a dissertation chapter, journal article, or other such academic paper. ·         Two reference letters: The reference letters should address the applicant’s research and the broader significance of this research for higher education and research in Hellenic Studies. These letters are to be submitted by the reference writers through the Interfolio site. For details on Yale’s Hellenic Studies program, please consult its website: https://macmillan.yale.edu/hellenic To apply, click here: https://apply.interfolio.com/195786 All inquiries should be addressed to: hellenic@yale.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195794": {
      "title": "Surgery, Trauma, Acute Care Surgeon, YNHH, Associate/ Professor (2420)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Surgery: Trauma, General Surgery and Surgical Critical Care Location: New Haven, CT  The Division of General Surgery, Trauma and Surgical Critical Care, at the Yale School of Medicine is seeking an academically oriented and qualified surgeon to join our faculty.  The position is at the Associate Professor or Professor level. Yale-New Haven Hospital is a 1541-bed ACS verified Level 1 Trauma Center for both adult and pediatric patients, and the major tertiary referral center for Southern Connecticut. The Trauma Service admits 4,800 patients per year.  The General Surgery Service evaluates approximately 4,200 patients and performs approximately 3,200 surgical procedures annually. We provide Surgical Critical Care services to most major surgical disciplines.  Clinical leadership opportunities are available, commensurate with experience. New Haven is conveniently located between Boston and New York. It is a great place to live, work, do business and enjoy life. There are ample opportunities to live within 20 minutes on the coast or in surrounding villages with small town charm. The greater New Haven area offers some of the top public and private schools in the country. The salary ranges listed below reflect the anticipated compensation range for all ranks included in this recruitment. $435,000 - $625,000 Qualified candidates will have completed fellowship training and be Board Certified in both General Surgery and Surgical Critical Care. In addition, candidates should have excellent clinical and interpersonal skills and the commitment to make important contributions to the field of Acute Care Surgery. Applicants should send a cover letter and curriculum vitae and three letters of reference. All application materials should be submitted electronically to: https://apply.interfolio.com/195794 Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Should you have any questions, please contact: Name: Kimberly A. Davis, MD, MBA, FACS, FCCM Professor of Surgery and Section Chief Division of General Surgery, Trauma and Surgical Critical Care Yale University School of Medicine Department of Surgery 330 Cedar Street, Boardman Building, 310 P.O. Box 208062 New Haven, CT 06520-8062 Tel# 203-785-3494/ Fax: 203-785-3950 Kimberly.davis@yale.edu Technical Difficulties with Interfolio: andrea.paolella@yale.edu ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Yale University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer. Yale values diversity among its students, staff, and faculty and strongly welcomes applications from women, persons with disabilities, protected veterans, and underrepresented minorities."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195652": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor Stroke Neurology (Bridgeport Hospita) (RFP 202510-2070)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Neurology Location: Fairfield County  The Department of Neurology at the Yale School of Medicine seeks to hire a vascular neurologist at the rank of Assistant/ Associate Professor to complement our services at Yale New Haven Health Bridgeport Hospital in Bridgeport, CT.  Rank will be based on education and experience. The position is composed of 24 service weeks (Monday – Sunday) and will include evaluation and management of stroke patients admitted through the emergency room and in-hospital. We anticipate faculty applicants to be dedicated to excellence in patient care, the highest quality and safety standards, as well as education of the next generation of neurologists. The vascular neurologist will join the ladder faculty at Yale School of Medicine and a team of stroke specialists and other subspecialty-trained neurologists providing advanced neurologic care using a patient-centered multidisciplinary approach.  We provide a highly collaborative, supportive, and individually mentored environment that promotes specialists to pursue career growth within their specific area of interest. Rank will be based on education and experience. We anticipate faculty applicants to be dedicated to the education of the next generation of subspecialty experts. It is an exciting time to join Yale Neurology with the upcoming opening of The Adams Neurosciences Center at Yale New Haven Hospital. The ANC will serve as an integrated inpatient academic hub for the diagnosis, treatment, and investigation of complex neurological and neurosurgical disorders and serve as a major access point of patients requiring continuing outpatient care. The community of New Haven and the surrounding areas provides a beautiful landscape to settle which has something for everyone including music, theater, a foodies’ delight, beaches, boating, shoreline towns, and tons of outdoor adventure. Yale University further offers the benefits of the academic environment, collegiate events, literary and art collections, and performance art. Proximity to New York, Boston, and the rest of New England offers additional opportunities, and we are happy to work with faculty to provide opportunities to practice in locations that meet their needs. We seek motivated academics with clinical expertise in:  1) providing acute stroke evaluations for patients in Fairfield county 2) providing inpatient stroke care to the consult service at  Bridgeport Hospital, 3) providing specialized stroke clinics through Yale Medicine, 4) providing acute stroke evaluations through the Yale New Haven Telestroke Program, 5) teaching residents, fellows, and medical students, and 6) participating in clinical research activities and collaborating on research projects.  This position offers applicants an exciting opportunity for individual programmatic development and goal discovery across varied academic interests within our division. It is anticipated that this position will be located predominantly at Bridgeport Hopsital in Bridgeport, Connecticut. Candidates must have an MD or equivalent, be board certified/ board eligible in Neurology, and eligible for medical licensure in the State of Connecticut. The salary ranges listed below reflect the anticipated compensation range for all ranks included in this recruitment Assistant Professor:   $203,000 - $304,900 Associate Professor:  $228,516 - $304,900 Interested applicants should upload a brief statement of interest, current CV and three contact references to the following link: apply.interfolio.com/195652 For additional information, please contact YSMNeurology.facultyaffairs@yale.edu Review of applications will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194878": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Quantum Computing Theory and Algorithms",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Engineering and Applied Science: Computer Science Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Computer Science in the School of Engineering & Applied Science at Yale University and the Yale Quantum Institute invite applications for one tenure-track or tenured faculty position in the area of quantum computing theory and algorithms. Applications will be considered at all ranks (Assistant/Associate/Full Professor). The anticipated start date for the position is July 1, 2027. Quantum science and engineering is one of five top priority areas identified in the University's Science Strategy Report and we have a vibrant and expanding community both in the Yale Quantum Institute and described more broadly at Quantum at Yale. Yale is engaged in a campus-wide investment in quantum science, engineering, and materials. Along with multiple appointments in these areas, the University has committed to, and has begun construction of, a new Physical Science and Engineering Building which will provide state-of-the-art facilities in quantum science, including quantum information science and quantum computing. The successful candidate is expected to lead a vibrant research program, be an engaged member of the Computer Science Department, and will be expected to teach in the undergraduate and graduate curriculum. We are especially interested in candidates who can foster cross-disciplinary collaborations, including, but not limited to, those with the Yale Quantum Institute. Yale offers a stimulating intellectual environment with exceptional opportunities for groundbreaking research, interdisciplinary collaboration, and impactful teaching. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Computer Science or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Candidates should have a strong and impactful publication record in theoretical computer science and/or quantum information theory venues. Candidates seeking a tenured appointment should also have a demonstrated track record of leadership, and evidence of substantial contributions to the field. Applications should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and teaching statement. Candidates seeking an appointment without tenure should arrange for three confidential letters of recommendation to be submitted via Interfolio. Candidates seeking an appointment with tenure are not required to provide recommendation letters at this time. The teaching statement should describe the candidate’s teaching experience and explain how they would contribute to the undergraduate and graduate curriculum at Yale. To ensure full consideration, please submit all materials by December 1, 2026. Review of applications will begin after December 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Contact Alicia Vignola (alicia.vignola@yale.edu) with any questions regarding the application."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195544": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor General Neurology (RFP 202610-2414)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Neurology Location: Greenwich Connecticut Faculty in the Division of General Neurology evaluate and treat all neurological conditions. They are the major access point for care for patients with neurological conditions across the region.  With the launch of the Brain Health Initiative, the Neurology department is committed to comprehensive neurologic care across the lifespan, access to groundbreaking research and the latest therapies.  The division includes faculty practices as well as the Yale Neurology Resident Clinics. The Resident Clinics offer specialized care in general neurology and stroke and includes the Neurology Urgent Access Clinic (NUAC). The goal of NUAC is to allow expedited evaluation of patients seen in the emergency room as well as those referred from physicians requiring an urgent assessment.  Yale Neurology has also recently developed a resident track in general neurology, focused on the comprehensive training of residents dedicated to providing the highest level of outpatient care across the spectrum of neurological disorders.  The Division of General Neurology is embedded within the Neurology department with the breadth of subspecialty care. We provide a highly collaborative, supportive, and mentored environment that promotes specialists to pursue career growth within their specific area of interest. This position offers applicants an exciting opportunity for individual programmatic development and goal discovery across varied academic interests within our division and department. Rank will be based on education and experience. We anticipate faculty applicants to be dedicated to the education of the next generation of master clinicians, educators, and researchers. It is an exciting time to join Yale Neurology with the upcoming opening of The Adams Neurosciences Center at Yale New Haven Hospital. The ANC will serve as an integrated inpatient academic hub for the diagnosis, treatment, and investigation of complex neurological and neurosurgical disorders and serve as a major access point of patients requiring continuing outpatient care. Anchored in advanced translational research and multidisciplinary clinical expertise, the Center will provide comprehensive, evidence-based care across the full spectrum of medical and surgical therapies. Through the integration of clinical innovation, emerging technologies, and collaborative research, the Center will advance scientific discovery while delivering highly individualized treatment strategies aimed at improving patient outcomes in disorders of the brain and nervous system. We seek motivated academic physicians with expertise in: 1) the care of adult general neurology patients, 2) participation in clinical trials, 3) opportunities to teach and mentor. Practice locations are located in Greenwich, Connecticut. Candidates must have an MD or equivalent and be board certified/board eligible in Neurology and may have subspecialty fellowship training.  Applicants must meet the criteria for medical licensure in the State of Connecticut. Faculty rank will be based on experience. The salary ranges listed below reflect the anticipated compensation range for all ranks included in this recruitment Assistant Professor:   $203,000 - $304,900 Associate Professor:  $228,516 - $304,900 Interested applicants should submit a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, and three contact references by applying to the location of interest below: apply.interfolio.com/195544"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195125": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Robotics",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Engineering and Applied Science: Mechanical Engineering Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Mechanical Engineering at Yale University invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant Professor. Candidates should strengthen the department’s research activities in the field of robotics and mechatronics. Example sub-fields of interest include, but are not limited to, candidates working on the mechanics and physics foundations of robotics, and on the broad applications of robotics, including in medicine and rehabilitation, manufacturing and industrial automation, space, or as experimental models in physics, biology, and ecology. We expect the successful candidate to establish a dynamic research group, be an interactive member of the faculty, participate in interdisciplinary research and training, and excel in teaching.  The successful candidate will be expected to teach at the  graduate and undergraduate level in the mechanical engineering curriculum. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. Candidates must have a Ph.D., or equivalent degree, in a relevant discipline at the time of hire, and an outstanding record of research that demonstrates originality in addressing significant questions in the field. Applicants should create a profile through Interfolio and upload a cover letter, statement of research plans, teaching plans (including a list of courses that they are qualified to teach), curriculum vitae, and up to three reprints of published work(s). Applicants should also arrange for three references to upload their letters of recommendation. Applications must be received by December 1, 2026. Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position, please email Samantha Leigh Torres at Samantha.L.Torres@yale.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194803": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor and Curator, Paleobotany",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: Faculty of Arts and Sciences: Physical Science: Earth and Planetary Sciences Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences (EPS) and the Yale Peabody Museum (YPM) at Yale University invite applications for a tenure-track faculty appointment at any rank (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) in the area of Paleobotany. The successful applicant to this faculty position will also fulfill the role of Curator-in-Charge of the Yale Peabody Museum’s Division of Paleobotany. A start date of July 2027 or later is anticipated. We seek candidates whose research addresses major questions about the evolutionary and/or ecological history of plant life and its impacts on the Earth system and who will use and expand the scope of the paleobotany collections of the YPM. We seek candidates who will develop an outstanding research program and teaching portfolio closely tied to the YPM collections and will enhance existing strengths of the Department, the Museum, and the University. The successful applicant will pursue independent, externally funded research; lead the YPM’s Division of Paleobotany; advise students; facilitate interdisciplinary research; and teach courses in paleobotany and related areas. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. or equivalent degree at the time of hire. Applicants should submit (1) a letter of application, (2) a curriculum vitae including a full list of publications, (3) a statement of research interests, and (4) a statement of interests in teaching, mentoring, and broader impacts. Broader impacts may include, for example, public education and outreach activities including development of museum exhibits, general-interest lectures, and educational tools, efforts to strengthen academic communities, and other positive societal benefits. Candidates seeking an appointment without tenure should arrange to have three confidential letters of recommendation submitted via Interfolio. Applications should be submitted online at apply.interfolio.com/194803 . Those that arrive before November 15, 2026 will receive full consideration. For information regarding the Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences, please visit http://earth.yale.edu ; additional information regarding the Yale Peabody Museum can be found at http://peabody.yale.edu . Questions regarding the application process can be addressed to Prof. Bhart-Anjan Bhullar, chair of the search committee, bhart-anjan.bhullar@yale.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195592": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor: Psychiatric Emergency Services",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Psychiatry Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Psychiatry, Yale School of Medicine seeks a psychiatrist to serve on Yale-New Haven Psychiatric Hospital (YNHPH) Psychiatric Emergency Services. Yale-New Haven Hospital is the major teaching and clinical research facility of the Yale University School of Medicine and consistently ranks among the best hospitals in the United States. Yale-New Haven Hospital encompasses the Yale-New Haven Psychiatric Hospital a 118 bed, acute care, psychiatric facility that consists of emergency services, inpatient programs, specialized step-down intensive outpatient/day hospital programs for adolescents and adults as well as Interventional Psychiatric Services. As an attending in Psychiatric Emergency Services, the candidate will be responsible for managing the multidisciplinary evaluation of patients presenting for emergency psychiatric evaluation.  In this capacity, you will work collaboratively with a team of nurses, social workers, psychiatric residents, and psychiatric APRNs.  Providing education to medical students and residents regarding diagnosis, medication management, treatment planning and care of patients in the acute phase of illness is a key component of the position.  Rewarding clinical and academic opportunities abound. The position will be a combination of day, night, and weekend hours, offering 20% of academic/administrative protected time (scheduled for 32 clinical hours & 8 hours of administrative time, per 40-hour work week). This position carries a faculty appointment within the Department of Psychiatry at the assistant or associate professor level depending upon the candidate’s qualifications and experience. Yale University offers Yale Signature Benefits, including but not limited to: ·         Medical coverage through Yale Health and Aetna ·         Dental coverage through Delta Dental ·         Vision coverage options through Eyemed ·         Yale University Retirement Account Plan ·         Paid time off ·         Disability coverage ·         Life insurance ·         Mortgage loan program ·         Commuter benefits ·         Yale Homebuyer program ·         College tuition benefit plan Yale ·         Advantage discount program (Discounts for Activities, Travel, services, shopping, etc.) Living in/near New Haven New Haven is recognized as a city of innovation, culture, diversity, and prosperity. It is located between Boston and New York on Long Island Sound, and has outstanding cultural amenities (museums, theater, music, and sports), award-winning restaurants (including several of the nation’s best pizza parlors), boutique shops and national brands, quaint neighborhoods and bustling downtown areas with a variety of living options. New Haven is accessible by plane, train, and automobile, and is easy to navigate on foot, by bicycle, or bus. A free shuttle service provided by Yale covers many areas in the city. Additional Information about New Haven, CT: Living in New Haven The salary range listed below reflects the anticipated compensation range for all ranks included in this recruitment: $280,000 - $300,000 Candidate must be board-certified in Psychiatry. To apply to this position please use the following link apply.interfolio.com/195592 Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you have questions about these job opportunities, please contact Hun Millard, Vice Chief of Psychiatry, via email at hun.millard@yale.edu for more information."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195325": {
      "title": "Orthopaedics & Rehabilitation – Adult Reconstruction/Arthroplasty- Assistant/Associate Professor (RFP-2413)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Orthopaedics and Rehabilitation Location: New Haven, CT Yale Orthopaedics & Rehabilitation together with Yale New Haven Hospital is ranked #41 in the nation by US News and World Report 2024-2025 for orthopedics.  The hospital is the only one in Connecticut to receive a national ranking in this specialty and is recognized for its expertise in procedures like hip fracture, back surgery, hip replacement, and knee replacement. We take pride in delivering exceptional quality and value-driven patient care.  We are committed to best practices in recruiting, supporting, mentoring, and sponsoring highly qualified and talented individuals. Our dedication to collaborative excellence is embodied by teams working closely together and using open, respectful communication to shape an interconnected, engaged, and productive community, which in turn allows individuals and innovation to thrive. Our commitment to collaborative excellence is integral to enhancing the health and lives of those in New Haven and our regional, national and global communities. Join our growing faculty.  The Department of Orthopaedics & Rehabilitation seeks an Orthopaedic Arthroplasty surgeon for a full-time faculty position with interest and expertise in Orthopaedic Adult Reconstruction. This position will have an expectation of clinical service commitment at the Yale New Haven Health Hospital System. Responsibilities: ·         Primary Practice Location: New Haven, CT ·         Participate in the training of residents, medical students, and other allied health students. ·         Engage in research activities, depending on the wishes and skill set of the candidate. Research is supported and valued. ·         Physicians with a sole focus on clinical care are welcome to apply. Opportunities: Academic appointment at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor, commensurate with experience. Contribution to program and practice development, medical leadership, teaching, and research. Representing the Department by participating in committees, conferences, and exemplifying the institution at professional meetings and community speaking engagements. · Applicants must be board-certified or board-eligible · Fellowship Training in Arthroplasty/Adult Reconstruction Apply with your CV, and a cover letter outlining your qualifications and interests."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194947": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Public Health Location: New Haven, Connecticut The Yale School of Public Health is seeking an Associate Research Scientist to join a multidisciplinary research program focused on improving maternal, adolescent, and community health in American Samoa. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr. Nicola Hawley and collaborators at Yale and in American Samoa, engaging regularly with project partners virtually and making occasional site visits to support research and programmatic activities as needed. A major focus of the position will be leadership of two newly funded randomized controlled trials in American Samoa: one evaluating the implementation and scale-up of a group prenatal care intervention and a second focused on improving adolescent mental health through a community-based intervention. The Associate Research Scientist may also contribute to other ongoing studies within the research program, depending on project needs, expertise, and scientific interests. The Associate Research Scientist will have substantial responsibility for translating study protocols into effective research operations and ensuring rigorous implementation of the trials. The position will also provide opportunities to contribute to implementation science, intervention adaptation, data analysis, and dissemination of findings through scientific presentations and publications. Responsibilities will include: · Providing scientific and operational leadership for two randomized controlled trials conducted in American Samoa. · Leading study start-up activities, including development and refinement of study protocols, standard operating procedures, implementation plans, and study workflows. · Working closely with investigators and the American Samoa research team to support high-quality implementation of study protocols. · Overseeing intervention implementation, including assessment of fidelity, documentation of adaptations, and identification of opportunities to strengthen intervention delivery. · Monitoring participant recruitment, retention, intervention engagement, and overall study performance and developing strategies to address emerging challenges. · Overseeing data quality and routinely reviewing study performance and data-quality reports. · Troubleshooting operational and implementation challenges in collaboration with study investigators and the American Samoa research team. · Supporting regulatory activities, protocol amendments, and other study modifications in collaboration with investigators and regulatory staff. · Contributing to implementation science activities, including assessment of implementation outcomes and examination of factors influencing intervention delivery, adaptation, and sustainability. · Contributing to analytic planning and interpretation of quantitative and qualitative study data. · Leading and contributing to scientific abstracts, presentations, manuscripts, and other research products. · Participating in grant reporting, development of future research proposals, and other activities supporting the broader research program. · Contributing, as appropriate, to other ongoing maternal, child, adolescent, cardiometabolic, or community health studies within the research portfolio. · Supporting mentorship and scientific development of research staff, trainees, and students involved in the projects. Applicants must have a doctoral degree (PhD, DrPH, ScD, MD, or equivalent) in epidemiology, public health, behavioral or social sciences, implementation science, or a closely related field. The successful candidate should have: · Experience conducting intervention or clinical research, preferably including randomized controlled trials or pragmatic/community-based intervention studies. · Strong organizational skills and demonstrated ability to coordinate complex, multi-component research projects. · Experience developing or implementing research protocols and study procedures. · Strong quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods research skills and experience contributing to scientific analysis and publication. · Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to work effectively with multidisciplinary and geographically distributed teams. · A demonstrated commitment to collaborative and community-engaged research. Experience in implementation science, maternal and child health, adolescent mental health, research conducted in Pacific Island communities or other under-resourced settings, and/or culturally adapted interventions is desirable but not required. The successful candidate will join a collaborative research environment at the Yale School of Public Health with opportunities to contribute intellectually to ongoing studies, develop independent areas of scholarship, participate in publications and future grant development, and collaborate with investigators across Yale and partner institutions. Applicants should submit the following through Interfolio: 1.     Curriculum vitae; 2.     Cover letter describing relevant research experience and interest in the position; 3.     A brief statement of research interests; and 4.     Contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Salary will be commensurate with experience and qualifications and will follow applicable Yale University guidelines."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188397": {
      "title": "ASCEND - Faculty Collaboration Grants (2027)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: Office of the Provost Location: New Haven, CT As part of the ASCEND Initiative , faculty collaboration grants are meant to support projects conducted by at least one HBCU faculty member and one Yale faculty member who - in collaboration - conduct research that transcends institutional boundaries, encourages interdisciplinary inquiry, and contributes collective expertise to global challenges. These awards encourage faculty to combine their perspectives and scholarly strengths to create novel insights and outcomes. Project scale can vary: proposals are welcome for “seed” projects to be developed as prototypes for a larger-scale future projects, or for projects that would be fully complete within the two-year period, or they can be somewhere in between.  Project scope can vary: proposals can be for academic and applied research projects, community-based research projects, and public policy related projects, and projects can engage a broad range of materials and sources. Projects across all fields are welcome - arts, humanities, social sciences, sciences, professional disciplines, their intersections, and beyond. Eligibility Projects must be led by at least two faculty members: one from an HBCU partner institution and one from Yale. Proposals including student and postdoc participation are encouraged. Award The maximum award per project is $125,000 in total over 1-2 years. Each proposal should be developed jointly by the HBCU and Yale faculty members, and should include these components: A letter from the HBCU's Office of Sponsored Research (or equivalent authorized grants administration office) indicating that the Office has reviewed and approved this proposal for submission. The letter should include the proposal title and name of collaborators and name, title, contact information, and signature of the designated Program Director from the Office of Sponsored Resarch. T he project title, summary statement, and the names of lead faculty from each institution:  provide a short summary giving a unique, relevant, and intriguing description of your research plan. (250 words, maximum) Project abstract: summarize the project’s goals. Describe the research plan, methods, and source materials that will be used to achieve the goals. (500 words, maximum) Project timeline: list the key project dates including the start and end dates as well as major milestones. Relevance statement: what benefits or outcomes will this project contribute to the academic field, the community, public policy, or other human interests? (500 words, maximum) Resources: what existing resources (to which access is already available) will participants in this project use? What resources - not currently available - will be needed, and how will participants gain access? How will these resources be used? (500 words, maximum) Each proposal should be developed jointly by the HBCU and Yale faculty members, and should include these components: The project title, summary statement, and the names of lead faculty from each institution:  provide a short summary giving a unique, relevant, and intriguing description of your research plan. (250 words, maximum) Estimated Budget: What is the total cost of this project, and how will the funds be used? Please provide a detailed breakdown of estimated expenses, including: A year-by-year breakdown of the total project budget A breakdown of expenses allocated to each institution (in part or in full) for each year Allowable expenses include compensation for relevant personnel (e.g., postdocs, student workers), materials, services, and course releases (with institutional approval from the HBCU partner). All expenditures must follow Yale’s policy on the appropriate use of university research funds. Abbreviated project abstract: If funded, this abstract will be featured on the university website. Please provide a two-sentence summation of the project significance, goals, and outcomes. One-page summary CV statement for each faculty member involved. Any other documents you wish to share. Submitting the Proposal Proposal applications should be submitted via Interfolio no later than January 15, 2027. Awards will be announced on April 19, 2027."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188406": {
      "title": "ASCEND - Faculty Research Fellowship (Summer 2027)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: Office of the Provost Location: New Haven, CT As part of the ASCEND Initiative , HBCU faculty at partner institutions may apply for summer or one-semester funded fellowships to conduct research at Yale in collaboration with one or more Yale faculty members. Fellows will be appointed to the appropriate academic department, program, institute, or center at Yale; will attend talks and seminars; participate in the vibrant co-curricular programs on campus; have access to the campus collections and libraries; and be in residence at Yale throughout the fellowship. Research Projects Projects are welcome from across a broad range of topics, such as academic and applied research projects, community-based research projects, public policy related projects, and arts and performance projects. Projects across all fields are encouraged - arts, humanities, social sciences, sciences, professional disciplines, their intersections, and beyond. Timeline Summer 2027 fellowships: applications will be accepted beginning October 1, 2026, and are due January 15, 2027. Awards will be announced February 26, 2027. Funding Summer fellowships are for two months. Fellows will receive a stipend equal to 2/9ths (2 months) of their base HBCU salary, plus a stipend for housing and travel, paid directly to the fellow. Eligibility Full-time faculty appointed at an HBCU partner institution are eligible to apply for a fellowship. To be eligible to accept a fellowship award, the faculty member’s dean or provost must approve the arrangement. Expectations for Fellows This is a residential fellowship and fellows are expected to engage with the vibrant academic life at Yale. Fellows will be invited to university events, lectures, programs, and are expected to spend the majority of the fellowship on campus. Submitting the Proposal Proposal applications should be submitted via Interfolio no later than January 15, 2027 . All application materials must be submitted through Interfolio by the deadline specified above.  Applications must include: 1. An application form 2. The applicant’s curriculum vitae (CV) 3. Letter of support from department chair or academic dean. 4. A research proposal (1500 words, maximum), including: Title of the research project Summary of the project’s goals and scope, describing the research plan, methods, and source materials that will be used to achieve the goals, and how much of the project will be completed during the fellowship. Relevance statement: what benefits or outcomes will this project contribute to the academic field, the community, public policy, or other human interests? Resources: what resources at Yale will you use to pursue this project? Support: what support might you need from Yale to help you accomplish this project? Collapse Instructions"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192177": {
      "title": "Advanced Imaging Cardiologist - ANC Tower Phase II",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Internal Medicine: Cardiology Location: New Haven, CT Position Description The Section of Cardiovascular Medicine at the Yale School of Medicine is seeking a full-time Noninvasive Cardiologist at the Assistant or Associate Professor level to support our advanced imaging program. This position offers an exciting opportunity to join a nationally recognized academic cardiology program dedicated to excellence in patient care, medical education, and clinical research. The successful candidate will contribute to a dynamic clinical practice while participating in the teaching and mentoring of medical students, residents, advanced practice providers and fellows within a collaborative academic environment. Primary Responsibilities · Provide comprehensive cardiovascular care in inpatient and outpatient settings. · Perform and interpret advanced, non-invasive cardiovascular imaging studies including echocardiography (TTE/TEE), cardiac MRI, and coronary CTA. · Participate in consultative cardiology services and on-call rotations. · Contribute to subspecialty services (e.g., advanced heart failure, imaging, electrophysiology, interventional cardiology—based on expertise) · Teach and mentor trainees, including medical students, residents, advanced practice providers and fellows. · Engage in scholarly activity appropriate to career track (clinical research, clinical trials, quality improvement, medical education innovation, or translational collaboration). · Participate in institutional and departmental career development opportunities. About Yale School of Medicine & Cardiology The Yale School of Medicine is one of the nation’s oldest, and most prestigious medical schools. The Section of Cardiovascular Medicine, within the Department of Internal Medicine, has more than 130 full-time ladder track faculty, engaged in clinical, education and research missions. In partnership with Yale New Haven Health System, our faculty provide comprehensive clinical care for cardiovascular conditions, with subspecialties including cardiovascular genetics, echocardiography, electrophysiology, heart failure, inherited cardiomyopathy, interventional cardiology (structural and vascular heart disease, peripheral vascular disease, coronary heart disease), and transplantation. The Section oversees a world-class training program, which includes more than 50 fellows, training in general cardiovascular medicine, as well as subspecialty training in advanced heart failure and transplantation, electrophysiology, interventional cardiology (coronary interventions, peripheral vascular interventions, and structural heart disease), cardiac Imaging and cardio-oncology, in addition to several research fellowships. Our cardiologists are actively engaged in cutting edge collaborative research, spanning basic science, translational research and clinical trials. The section fosters close collaboration with departments across the university, supporting discovery in areas such as genomics, artificial intelligence and data science, biomedical engineering, and health outcomes while providing robust mentorship and training opportunities for fellows, early-career faculty, and physician-scientists committed to advancing cardiovascular medicine. Specialized research centers within the Section include Yale Cardiology Research Group (YCRG), Yale Translational Research Imaging Center (YTRIC), Cardiovascular Clinical Trials Program, the Center for Outcomes Research and Evaluation (CORE), and the Yale Cardiovascular Research Center (CVRC). About Yale New Haven Health System’s Heart and Vascular Center (HVC) Yale New Haven Health System is Connecticut’s leading healthcare system, which includes 5 hospitals with nearly 2700 beds, supported by more than 7500 university and community physicians and advanced practice providers. Yale New Haven Hospital Heart and Vascular Center (HVC) treats more heart and vascular patients than any other facility in Connecticut. The multidisciplinary team of physicians, nurses and other professionals are dedicated to providing our patients with excellence in cardiac care. With a history of pioneering cardiology specialists and innovation that includes cardiology firsts, Yale New Haven Hospital is one of the nation’s leading providers of heart and vascular services, offering the most advanced technology and facilities. Successful candidates must be fellowship-trained and either Board Certified or Board Eligible in both cardiovascular medicine and advanced cardiac imaging. Candidates must meet criteria for appointment as an Assistant or Associate Professor of Medicine in the Academic Clinician (AC) or Clinician Educator Scholar Track (CES). Candidates must have an active unrestricted medical license in Connecticut or be eligible for licensure. Candidates with additional training, experience and interest in subspecialty cardiology are encouraged to apply. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For more information on this position, or any other positions with Yale Cardiovascular Medicine, please contact cvmrecruiting@yale.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191722": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Center for Neuroethology and Physiological Plasticity",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Basic Science -YSM: Cellular & Molecular Physiology Location: New Haven, CT Faculty Position — Assistant Professor The Center for Neuroethology and Physiological Plasticity ( https://medicine.yale.edu/neuroethology-physiological-plasticity/ ) and the Department of Cellular and Molecular Physiology ( https://medicine.yale.edu/physiology/ ) at Yale are conducting a faculty search at the assistant professor level. The Center harnesses the extraordinary adaptations of specialized vertebrate organisms to uncover fundamental biological principles driving the plasticity and resilience of neurophysiological, metabolic, sensory, and reproductive systems—principles with relevance to human health, aging, and therapeutic innovation that would remain hidden in conventional laboratory models. We seek creative investigators whose research program aligns with the Center’s mission. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying sensory processing, neural and physiological plasticity, metabolic regulation, or reproductive physiology. Candidates must hold a Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent degree. Applicants should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a 2-page statement that describes past research accomplishments and future goals and should arrange three letters of reference. Applicants should apply at the following website: https://apply.interfolio.com/191722. Applications will be reviewed as they are received until the positions are filled, with priority given to those applications received by November 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190528": {
      "title": "Emergency Medicine Faculty Positions at the Assistant and Associate Professor Ranks",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Emergency Medicine Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Emergency Medicine is seeking faculty members at the Assistant and Associate Professor ranks to provide clinical care, education, research, mentorship, and service within the department. Faculty members will provide high-quality clinical care in Emergency Medicine, ensuring appropriate clinical coverage and care that meets established standards of practice. Responsibilities will include teaching in clinical settings, contributing to educational programs in Emergency Medicine, and developing and delivering educational content within their area of expertise. The successful candidate will engage in scholarly activities, including developing independent, externally funded research programs, collaborating with faculty at Yale and other institutions on research initiatives and academic projects, and contributing to advancements in the field of Emergency Medicine. Faculty will also provide mentorship to Yale medical students and residents, supporting their development and involvement in academic and research projects. Additional responsibilities include participation in departmental administrative activities and service efforts that support the mission of the Department of Emergency Medicine. Candidates will enter at the Assistant/Associate level, commensurate with experience and credentials. Candidates must be residency-trained and board-certified or board-eligible in emergency medicine Please apply at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/190528."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185235": {
      "title": "Anesthesiology, General, NORA, Assistant/Associate Professor (2192)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Anesthesiology Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Anesthesiology is seeking an Assistant or Associate Professor level Anesthesiologist to join our team in the Division of General Anesthesia. Clinical assignments may include Adams Neurosciences Center (ANC), Saint Raphael Campus (SRC), and/or Yale New Haven Hospital (YNHH) with the possibility of other locations as needed. Faculty will be involved in providing clinical care, including non-operating room anesthesia, as well as resident and fellow education. There is also an opportunity for clinical and translational research, as well as outcomes related research.  In the event that you have formal post-residency subspecialty training, some assignment to that area will be made if feasible and desirable to the candidate. Candidates must be Board Certified / Board Eligible and qualify for a Connecticut license. Candidates in subspecialty areas must be fellowship trained. In addition, candidates should have excellent clinical and interpersonal skills and the commitment to make important contributions to the field of Anesthesia. Interested candidates should apply using the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/185235 Questions specific to this position may be sent to lisa.leffert@yale.edu. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/184551": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, General Neurology, (RFP # 202604-2224)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Neurology Location: Fairfield  The Division of General Neurology evaluates and treats all neurological conditions. The division includes the Yale Headache Center, which specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of headaches, and the Yale Neurology Resident Clinics. The Resident Clinics offer specialized care in general neurology and stroke and include the Neurology Urgent Access Clinic (NUAC). The goal of NUAC is to allow expedited evaluation of patients seen in the emergency room as well as those referred from physicians requiring an urgent assessment. A major role of the division is to ensure patients are seen by appropriate sub-specialists within the department. If the diagnosis is unclear or the patient would benefit from sub-specialty care, then the General Neurology Division will initially evaluate patients and make internal referrals to the appropriate sub-specialists to allow for the best possible care. We seek motivated academic physicians with clinical expertise in: 1) the care of adult general neurology and subspecialty patients, 2) Participation in clinical trials, 3) Opportunity to teach. Practice location will be in Fairfield County. We anticipate faculty applicants to be dedicated to the education of the next generation of subspecialty experts. We provide a highly collaborative, supportive, and mentored environment that promotes specialists to pursue career growth within their specific area of interest. This position offers applicants an exciting opportunity for individual programmatic development and goal discovery across varied academic interests within our division and department. Rank will be based on education and experience. We anticipate faculty applicants to be dedicated to the education of the next generation of subspecialty experts. New England in spirit and cosmopolitan in sensibility, Greenwich is an affluent community located in beautiful Fairfield County, Connecticut. Greenwich not only boasts convenient rail access to New Haven and New York City, but it is also safe, family-friendly, and offers a multitude of local attractions, from beach access and sprawling parks to world-class shopping and dining. With just a short commute to Yale University, as well as to Yale Neurology’s clinic location, Fairfield is an ideal location to live, work, and play. Candidates must have an MD or equivalent and be board certified/board eligible in Neurology and have completed a fellowship in General Neurology.  Applicants must meet the criteria for medical licensure in the State of Connecticut. Faculty rank will be based on experience. Interested applicants should submit a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, and three contact references by applying the following link: apply.interfolio.com/184551 For additional information, please contact YSMNeurology.facultyaffairs@yale.edu Review of applications begins immediately and continues until the positions are filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183588": {
      "title": "Anesthesiology, OBGYN, General Assistant/Associate Professor (2167)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Anesthesiology Location: New Haven, CT The Department of Anesthesiology is seeking an Assistant or Associate Professor level Anesthesiologist to join our team in the Division of General Anesthesia. Clinical assignments may include Yale New Haven Hospital, Bridgeport Hospital, and/or Lawrence + Memorial Hospital, with the possibility of other locations as needed. Faculty will be involved in providing clinical care as well as resident and fellow education. There is also an opportunity for clinical and translational research, as well as outcomes related research. In the event that you have formal post-residency subspecialty training, some assignment to that area will be made if feasible and desirable to the candidate. Candidates must be Board Certified / Board Eligible and qualify for a Connecticut license. Candidates in subspecialty areas must be fellowship trained. In addition, candidates should have excellent clinical and interpersonal skills and the commitment to make important contributions to the field of Anesthesia. Interested candidates should apply using the following link: http://apply.interfolio.com/ 183588 Questions specific to this position may be sent to lisa.leffert@yale.edu. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/177689": {
      "title": "GEPN Clinical Faculty (Lecturer)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Nursing Location: Orange, CT  The Graduate Entry Pre-Specialty in Nursing (GEPN) program is a distinctive and pioneering model that has influenced similar initiatives at nursing schools nationwide. As the foundational pre-licensure registered nursing program within Yale School of Nursing, this one-year certificate program serves as a bridge for individuals who hold at least a bachelor’s degree in a non-nursing discipline and are preparing to pursue advanced practice nursing education. During this intensive first year, students complete all required didactic and clinical coursework to prepare for the NCLEX and transition into advanced nursing specialty education. The success and impact of the program are driven by the expertise, mentorship, and dedication of our clinical faculty. We are seeking dedicated and experienced clinical faculty to teach in our GEPN program. Clinical faculty will provide competency-based instruction, supervision, and evaluation of students’ competencies in clinical settings. Areas of need include: · Care of the Adult Patient (Fall semester) · Pediatrics (Spring Semester) · Care of Childbearing Families (Spring Semester) · Psychiatric-Mental Health (Summer Session) *Please note we are currently only accepting applications for Care of Childbearing Families and Pediatrics at this time. Job Description Clinical Faculty are appointed as a lecturer for the term during which they are hired. The responsibilities include, but are not limited to teaching, supervising, and evaluating pre-RN licensure nursing students during their clinical rotations. This role supports the development of students' clinical reasoning and judgement, professional identity, and competencies by providing hands-on instruction, guidance, and feedback while ensuring alignment with program outcomes and standards of professional nursing practice. Summary of Responsibilities · Support the integration of clinical competencies with didactic content by maintaining communication with course faculty and director of clinical education. · Facilitate student learning through direct patient care experiences, supervision, pre- and post-clinical conferences, reflective practice, and real-time teaching. · Evaluate student performance and competencies, provide timely feedback, and document clinical progress in accordance with program guidelines. · Maintain up-to-date knowledge of clinical protocols, evidence-based practices, nursing code of ethics, and scope of practice for registered nurses. · Ensure student compliance with professional clinical conduct expectations set forth by both the school of nursing and the clinical site, including, but not limited to, adherence to ethical and professional standards. · Attend program orientation. · Monitor and verify student compliance with weekly clinical hour requirements, ensuring accurate documentation and validation of completed hours. · Maintain open communication with course faculty and director of clinical education, promptly reporting any student concerns, support needs, or issues impacting clinical performance. · Foster an inclusive and supportive learning environment aligned with Yale School of Nursing’s mission. Minimum Qualifications · Registered Nurses with a master's degree or higher. Candidates with advanced practice certification, or a master's degree specializing in nursing education, leadership, or informatics are also encouraged to apply. · At least two years of recent relevant clinical experience. · Passion for teaching and mentoring students in clinical settings. · Experience in teaching in clinical settings or academic instruction is a plus. However, we will consider skilled and experienced clinicians who have precepted novice nurses or contributed to the orientation of new nurses. · Commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment aligned with Yale School of Nursing’s mission To apply for this position, please submit a curriculum vitae to Interfolio, via the link: https://apply.interfolio.com/177689. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue on a rolling basis. Please email your questions to Jennifer.McIntosh@yale.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/177460": {
      "title": "Anesthesiology, General, Instructor of Clinical Anesthesiology, LMH, AC (2075)",
      "description": "Department: Yale University: School of Medicine: Anesthesiology Location: New London, CT The Department of Anesthesiology is seeking an Anesthesiologist to join our team at our Lawrence & Memorial Hospital location. In the event that you have formal post-fellowship subspecialty training, some assignment to that area will be made feasible and desirable to the candidate. Candidates must be Board Certified / Board Eligible and qualify for a Connecticut license. Candidates in subspecialty areas must be fellowship trained. Interested candidates should apply using the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/177460 Questions specific to this position may be sent to lisa.leffert@yale.edu. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    }
  },
